35.085, 35.143, 35.178, 35.224, 35.271, 35.34, 35.398, 35.456, 35.514, 35.561, 35.607, 35.654, 35.7, 35.747, 35.781, 35.828, 35.886, 35.932, 35.99, 36.048, 36.083, 36.107, 36.141, 36.188, 36.234, 36.269, 36.304, 36.362, 36.42, 36.478, 36.501, 36.536, 36.594, 36.641, 36.675, 36.733, 36.768, 36.826, 36.873, 36.966, 37.012, 37.128, 37.279, 37.43]}, "quality_check": null, "script_sha256": "4340bdbe97d0a60e6c5cd07bf7688ca7f14aa7e8453a8a086ec27b3877757b88", "text": "Imagine losing one league game all season. One. You win thirty, finish with ninety-seven points, and still go home without the trophy. Then, three years later, you miss out by one point again. That is the standard Jurgen Klopp was fighting. And when he questioned the financial power on the other side of that fight, it was easy to hear an excuse. I think that was a mistake. Because you can admire Manchester City's football and still ask whether the competition around it was fair. Now, with the Premier League's findings against City, Klopp's warnings sound very different. But the thing that bothers me most is something no verdict can fix. You cannot give people those afternoons back.", "voice_id": "bu5eKETbFKC8G702EAU4", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2"}